# separate drawings of "the same fight, harder" had made a row read as three
# unrelated things. The drawn emblem is gone (fight nodes wear the real creature
# now), but the colour axis survives it and does the same job: it is the DISC
# behind the silhouette, so a row still reads as one question at three settings.
#
# That splits the row's two questions cleanly across two channels:
#   ICON COLOUR = how hard  (tier — green / blue / red, the colours the
#                            difficulty buttons already use)
#   RING COLOUR = how rich  (the node's reward grade, D grey → S gold)
# The intensity pips stay under the node as a non-colour fallback.
const TIER_COLORS := [
	Color(0.42, 0.85, 0.40),   # Normal  — green
	Color(0.38, 0.62, 0.98),   # Veteran — blue
	Color(0.94, 0.36, 0.32),   # Brutal  — red
]

# Reward marks reuse the icons the game ALREADY has — the item's own icon from
# EquipmentManager.ITEM_ICONS, the stat's icon from UnitFactory.STAT_ICONS, the
# coin from the drop system. A reward drawn with the same art the bag and the
# level-up screen use tells the player it's the same thing; a bespoke glyph
# would be a second vocabulary for no gain.
const COIN_ICON := "res://assets/UI/Vector_UI_Pack_dobo_ui/Icons/64px/coin_icon_64px.png"
# How big the reward badge on a fight node is, as a multiple of its original
# 10px-rim / 14px-icon size. Owner call (2026-09-22): "+150%" — the badge was
# the one piece of information on the node you still had to lean in to read.
const REWARD_BADGE_SCALE := 2.5
# Node size on maps with a 5-wide row (VD-14). The badge scales with it, since
# it is part of the node — see _layout().
const NODE_SCALE_WIDE := 0.85
# Every node, on every map (owner, 2026-09-24: "turn all nodes size down to
# .9x it just feels so cramped"). Stacks with NODE_SCALE_WIDE.
const NODE_SCALE_ALL := 0.9
# …and where it sits: pinned to the node's rim at the top-right, hanging mostly
# OUTSIDE the disc so it stops covering the creature. OUTSET is how far past the
# rim the badge's centre sits, in badge radii — 0 would straddle the rim half
# in / half out; 0.6 leaves only the inner fifth of it over the node.
const REWARD_BADGE_OUTSET := 0.6
const REWARD_BADGE_DIR := Vector2(0.6598, -0.7514)   # (0.72, -0.82) normalised

# ── MOTION (owner call, 2026-09-22: "make it look more professional") ────────
# Everything that moves on this screen runs off _clock, a screen-local time
# advanced only by _process. Not Time.get_ticks_msec(): a harness can then step
# the reveal, the flow and the travel beat frame by frame and get the same
# picture every run, which is what lets motion be checked at all.
#
# CURVED PATHS. A straight line between two circles reads as a flowchart; an
# S-curve reads as a trail. The curve is a cubic with horizontal tangents at
# both ends, and that shape is chosen for a reason beyond looks: every node in a
# row shares one x (layout_stage is per ROW), so every curve between the same two
# rows walks the same x at the same parameter, and its y is always the same
# weighted blend of its two endpoints. Two edges that don't cross as straight
# lines therefore can't cross as curves either — RunMap's no-crossing guarantee
# survives the redraw. map_smoke checks that on real layouts rather than trusting
# this paragraph.
const PATH_SEGMENTS := 18         # polyline resolution of one curved edge
const HOVER_LIFT := 0.08          # the node under the cursor grows by 8%
const LIFT_EASE := 14.0           # how fast a lift eases in and out, per second
# Dashes drifting forward along the lit cone: the dimming says WHERE you can go,
# the flow says which way the road runs.
const FLOW_DASH := 9.0
const FLOW_GAP := 13.0
const FLOW_SPEED := 34.0          # px per second, parent -> child
const FLOW_COLOR := Color(1.0, 0.97, 0.88, 0.8)   # owner: 0.55 -> 0.8 (2026-09-22)
# The map inks itself in once per biome, row by row out from the start node.
const REVEAL_ROW_DELAY := 0.07    # s between one row starting and the next
const REVEAL_NODE_POP := 0.30     # s for a node to scale in (with a small overshoot)
const REVEAL_EDGE_DRAW := 0.24    # s for an edge to draw itself to its child
# Playback speed of the whole reveal. The three timings above are its SHAPE — how
# rows stagger, pop and ink relative to each other — and this is its tempo, so
# slowing it down keeps the choreography instead of stretching one part of it.
# Owner call (2026-09-22): 1.0 read "a bit too fast", 0.7 is 30% slower.
const REVEAL_RATE := 0.7
# Choosing a node walks a marker along the path before the fight opens.
const TRAVEL_SECONDS := 0.55

# Fallback glyphs, used until the art above exists. Letterform placeholders on
# purpose: honest about being placeholders in a way a borrowed icon isn't.
const GLYPHS := {
	RunMap.NodeType.RELIC:    "◈",
	RunMap.NodeType.BATTLE:   "⚔",
	RunMap.NodeType.ELITE:    "☠",
	RunMap.NodeType.BOSS:     "✹",
	RunMap.NodeType.EVENT:    "?",
	RunMap.NodeType.MERCHANT: "$",
	RunMap.NodeType.CAMP:     "▲",
}

# Intensity is carried by the RING (how many marks), grade by its COLOUR, so
# "how hard" and "how rich" never fight over the same cue.
const TIER_NAMES := ["Skirmish", "Veteran", "Brutal"]

# Mini-board geometry. Only the ENEMY half is drawn (rows 0-3): your own side is
# yours to arrange and showing it empty would imply the preview knows where your
# heroes will be.
const BOARD_ROWS := 4
const BOARD_W := 630.0
const BOARD_H := 286.0
# Role only tints the little shadow under each body now — the sprite itself
# says what the unit is, which is the whole point of previewing the real art.
# This popup currently resolves to the dark framed plate used by the map (the
# rest of UITheme's text palette is brown-on-cream). Keep the dossier legible
# and restrained: warm white for facts, old gold for emphasis, smoke for labels.
# The road you have walked, and the one-edge preview of the road you are
# about to walk. Same hue: the preview is the same material, not a new state.
const ROAD_GOLD       := Color(1.0, 0.85, 0.35, 1.0)
const ROAD_GOLD_HOVER := Color(1.0, 0.93, 0.62, 1.0)

const TIP_TEXT := Color(0.93, 0.91, 0.84)
const TIP_GOLD := Color(0.86, 0.63, 0.22)
const TIP_MUTED := Color(0.66, 0.64, 0.59)

var _overlay:   ColorRect
var _backdrop:  TextureRect          # the biome plate behind the path
var _panel:     Panel
var _header:    TextureRect
var _panel_size: Vector2 = Vector2(PANEL_W, PANEL_H)
# Live layout, recomputed per present() from the panel's real size. The map is
# now viewport-sized and rows are 2-4 wide, so spacing can't be constant.
var _stage_gap: float = STAGE_GAP
var _lane_gap:  float = LANE_GAP
var _node_r:  float = NODE_R
var _node_scale: float = 1.0      # NODE_SCALE_WIDE on maps with a 5-wide row
var _title:     Label
var _sub:       Label
var _canvas:    Control              # edges are drawn here, nodes are its children
# Node under the cursor, "" when none. Only the edge CURRENT -> _hover_id ever
# lights from it, so hovering a far-off node previews nothing and can't be
# mistaken for a legal move.
var _hover_id: String = ""
# A modal can disappear with the cursor already sitting over a map node (the
# centre recruit card is the common case). That inherited position is not a
# hover decision. Previews arm only after deliberate pointer movement.
const HOVER_ARM_DISTANCE := 10.0
var _hover_armed: bool = false
var _hover_origin: Vector2 = Vector2.ZERO

# ── THE COMMITMENT PREVIEW ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hovering a node dims everything that node CANNOT reach. Owner call
# (2026-09-22), and it is the legibility half of the mercy-edge rule in
# RunMap._grant_relief_edges: that rule gives a stranded node a way out whenever
# the no-crossing geometry allows, but in roughly 0.4 maps per biome it can't,
# and the map stays pointed. A trap you can SEE is a choice; the same trap
# discovered two rows later is just a lost run.
#
# It is also what makes a lane commitment fair at the moment it's made. Slay the
# Spire's whole act is readable from floor 1, which is why walking into two
# elites there is something a player did on purpose. This map is fully drawn
# too, but nobody traces four rows of hairlines with their eyes — so the cone
# gets traced for them.
#
# Hovering ANY node works, not just the fork in front of you: scouting the route
# is already the reason the dossier shows fights anywhere on the map.
# While a cone is up these are ABSOLUTE, not multipliers on the depth fade.
# Owner call (2026-09-22): stacking the two made the reachable path read at
# ~70% and the rest at ~5%, so the cone looked washed out and the map looked
# empty. Depth dimming answers "how far ahead is this", which is the resting
# question; the moment a cone is up the only question is "can I get there",
# and that answer is not more or less true further along the route.
const CONE_LIT := 1.0           # anything the hovered node can reach
const CONE_DIM := 0.25          # …and everything it cannot
var _cone_id: String = ""       # which node _cone was computed for
var _cone: Dictionary = {}      # {node_id: true} — focused node plus all descendants

# ── HOVER LOOKS, CLICK ASKS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Owner call (2026-09-22). The dossier is a big centred card, so opening it on
# hover covered the very paths the cone had just lit — the two features fought
# each other and the card won. Now hover does nothing but light the cone, and
# the fight preview costs a CLICK.
#
# Clicking a node you can step onto a SECOND time enters it, and the card
# carries a BEGIN button that does the same thing, so nothing can start a fight
# without the preview having been on screen first. Clicking anywhere that is not
# a node and not the card puts it away.
#
# Any node can be clicked, not just the fork in front of you — scouting the
# route is the reason the card shows fights from anywhere on the map, and with
# hover no longer opening it, a click is the only way left to look ahead.
#
# ── AND RESTING ON A NODE ASKS FOR YOU ────────────────────────────────────────
# Keep the cursor still on one node for a second and its dossier opens by
# itself. The common case — sweep the forks, settle on one, go — is then ONE
# click, while sweeping still costs nothing but the cone. A card that opened
# this way is NOT pinned: arriving at another node puts it away again, so the
# map never accumulates a card you didn't ask for.
const DOSSIER_DWELL := 1.0      # seconds of stillness on a node

var _selected_id: String = ""   # the node whose dossier is open ("" = none)
var _dossier_pinned: bool = false   # opened by a click (stays) vs. by dwell (transient)
var _dwell: float = 0.0         # seconds the cursor has rested on _hover_id
var _tip_action: Button         # BEGIN — only shown on a node you may enter

var _clock: float = 0.0         # screen-local seconds; see MOTION above
var _lift: Dictionary = {}      # node_id -> eased lift 0..1 (absent = resting)
var _reveal_key: String = ""    # "biome:seed" of the last map that inked itself in
var _reveal_start: float = -1.0 # _clock when the reveal began; -1 = not revealing
var _travel: Dictionary = {}    # {from, to, node, t} while the marker walks; {} otherwise

